NCMAT v7
#
# Test file with an unusual VDOS, not intended for physics usage.
#
# Provenance:
#
#   Provided by Douglas D. DiJulio, who describes it as a hydrogen VDOS test
#   case with a 0.546 eV oscillator: the H2 translational spectrum with a
#   diffusion component of weight 0.005 removed, for usage at 14 K (inelastic
#   scattering only). VDOS values were rounded to 8 significant digits.
#
# Why it is a test case:
#
#   The VDOS has features which are a few meV wide (a soft mode), while
#   extending to 0.55eV (an oscillator peak which is 3 points wide and cut off
#   by the end of the grid). At low temperatures the Gn spectra of the phonon
#   expansion therefore extend over thousands of kT, while having structure on
#   the scale of kT. When NCrystal reduced the resolution of such spectra
#   based on their number of points only, cross sections at 14K were wrong by
#   50% (compared to results of NJOY).
#
#   Additionally, the VDOS rises steeply at the lowest energies (the value at
#   the first grid point is half of the maximum of the soft mode). At 14K this
#   gives G1 a narrow peak at E=0, with 60% of the weight within two grid bins
#   of E=0.
#
@DENSITY
  1 g_per_cm3
@DYNINFO
  element  H
  fraction 1
  type     vdos
